WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
BUKOLA AYODELE: Many apps
have a common boilerplate.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.200
It would be great
if there was a way

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.925
to reuse infrastructure
templates

00:00:05.925 --> 00:00:08.490
to deploy multiple services.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:12.360
Let's see how kpt
helps Bob manage this.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.280
Bob is a lead on the
application development team,

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.050
which is currently in a crunch.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.000
They need to launch 10 apps
for an upcoming deadline.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.120
Most of the effort is in
the common infrastructure,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.820
but each app requires
certain modifications

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.700
that are hard to manage
in a consistent way.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.750
Bob's first instinct is to copy
and paste Kubernetes manifests

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.370
between projects.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:37.380
But this has many downsides.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.220
Manually having to make
changes to copy-pasted code

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.130
is time-consuming
and error-prone.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:47.020
In an ideal world, Bob
would be able to define

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.410
standardized
configuration templates,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.710
customize setting for
each specific application,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.900
and then modify
them all as a unit.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.850
What Bob needs is packaging.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.020
And with deadlines looming,
he needs it pronto.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.750
Bob asks Charlie for
help, who introduces him

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.230
to Ida, a developer
on the platforms team.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:10.950
Ida explains the benefits
of config as data and shows

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:15.690
Bob how to use templates with
kpt to reuse customized project

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:20.760
boilerplate, which dramatically
accelerates Bob's work.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.290
Kpt is an OSS tool for
Kubernetes packaging, which

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.400
uses a standard format to
bundle, publish, customize,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.580
update, and apply
configuration manifests.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.520
Its git plus YAML
architecture means

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.350
it just works with
existing tools, frameworks,

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.230
and platforms.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:44.100
Kpt is built around a
config-as-data architecture

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.360
that bundles up Kubernetes
resource configuration.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.810
This format is readable for
both humans and machines,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.700
and is a source of truth
for a declarative platform.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.540
The benefit of
reading and writing

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.720
the package content using
standardized data structures

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.460
enables kpt to apply bulk
updates, creation, and deletion

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.460
of projects, run
user-defined functions

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.550
to apply custom behaviors
to config files,

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.670
seamlessly integrate into the
Kubernetes infrastructure,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.450
and more, which is exactly
what Bob's team needs.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.830
Say we wanted to work with
Kubernetes example repositories

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.120
for demo's sake.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.240
We could run the
kpt pkg get command

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.700
to fetch the entire directory.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.950
This has a lot of files.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.330
No fear, though.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.600
We can use the kpt
cfg count command

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.050
to summarize the files
in a easier way to read.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:43.290
You can do a lot more with
filtering and listing resource

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.270
in different ways, but
I want to take a second

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:50.650
to focus on using kpt to update
and validate config files.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.290
Let's take a look at an example.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.040
If we used the kpt cfg
list-setters command,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.390
we can see which values were
able to use the kpt tool.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.320
So this package
contains three setters

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.830
we can use with the kpt set
tool and the current value

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.330
of the properties.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:12.480
Let's set the number
of replicas to 3.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.030
Now one of the benefits
of a tool like kpt

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.360
is its ability to use
functions to apply changes

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.730
like this on a number
of config files at once.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:23.790
So I urge you to look
into the resource link

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.470
below for a walkthrough on that.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:27.450
And there you have it.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.710
With kpt, you can easily create,
and customize, and compose

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.080
configuration packages,
while adhering

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.320
to the configuration-as-data
philosophy.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.480
With this knowledge, Bob
is able to quickly get

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.520
his application spun up in
time to meet the deadline.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.070
So that's it for kpt.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.200
Make sure to check out
the description for links

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.240
to more resources
on it, and Like

